-- Q3 [Natural Selection & Fitness] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Natural Selection & Fitness', 'A researcher studying a population of Galápagos ground finches wants to quantify the Darwinian (relative) fitness of an individual bird. To do so, the researcher measures which property of that individual.', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Its surviving offspring relative to others in the population"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Its total lifespan compared with other individuals"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Its absolute number of offspring produced"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The chance fluctuation in its allele frequencies"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is A because relative (Darwinian) fitness is defined by an individual''s reproductive contribution to the next generation measured against the rest of the population, not by its own survival in isolation. OpenStax states that natural selection "selects for individuals with greater contributions to the gene pool of the next generation. Scientists call this an organism''s evolutionary (Darwinian) fitness," and clarifies that "it is not an individual''s absolute fitness that counts, but rather how it compares to the other organisms in the population. Scientists call this concept relative fitness, which allows researchers to determine which individuals are contributing additional offspring to the next generation." The glossary likewise defines relative fitness as an "individual''s ability to survive and reproduce relative to the rest of the population." (Choice A) is correct: surviving offspring measured against others in the population is exactly the comparative reproductive contribution that defines relative fitness. (Choice B) is wrong because fitness is a measure of reproduction, not longevity; an individual can be long-lived yet contribute few offspring, and the text frames fitness around "contributing additional offspring," not lifespan. (Choice C) is wrong because an absolute offspring count is the individual''s absolute fitness, which the text explicitly says is "not" what counts—relative fitness compares the individual to the rest of the population. (Choice D) is wrong because chance fluctuation in allele frequencies describes genetic drift, the "effect of chance on a population''s gene pool," a random force distinct from fitness-based selection. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the textbook definition of relative (Darwinian) fitness as a comparative measure of reproductive contribution.', 'easy', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
