-- Q5 [Natural Selection & Fitness] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Natural Selection & Fitness', 'In a beetle population exposed to an insecticide, an allele conferring insecticide resistance sits so close on the same chromosome to a recessive lethal allele that crossover almost never separates them. Despite the resistance allele''s clear survival advantage against the insecticide, its frequency falls over successive generations. This decline is best explained by the principle that natural selection acts on:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "rare alleles faster than common alleles"}, {"label": "B", "text": "the whole organism''s net fitness, not isolated alleles"}, {"label": "C", "text": "phenotypes through random sampling of gametes"}, {"label": "D", "text": "alleles entering a population through migration"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is B because natural selection screens individuals by their overall (net) Darwinian fitness, not by any single allele in isolation. OpenStax states that "Natural selection acts on entire organisms, not on an individual allele within the organism. An individual may carry a very beneficial genotype... but if that same individual also carries an allele that results in a fatal childhood disease, that... phenotype will not pass to the next generation because the individual will not live to reach reproductive age" (Biology 2e, lines 23151-23156). Because the beneficial resistance allele is tightly linked to a recessive lethal allele and crossover rarely separates them (linkage occurs when "genes... are located physically close to each other on the same chromosome," lines 15385-15386), individuals carrying the resistance allele also tend to carry the lethal allele, lowering their net fitness and dragging the beneficial allele down with it. (Choice A) is incorrect: selection does not preferentially target alleles by rarity; rarity slows the removal of recessive deleterious alleles ("it will not happen frequently enough for natural selection to... swiftly eliminate the allele," lines 22935-22937), the opposite of "faster." (Choice B) is correct: selection operates on the integrated organism''s relative reproductive success, so a beneficial allele linked to a lethal one can be lost (lines 23151-23156). (Choice C) is incorrect: random sampling of gametes describes genetic drift, the "effect of chance on a population''s gene pool" (lines 23389-23390), a non-selective force, not the fitness-based mechanism here. (Choice D) is incorrect: alleles entering through migration describe gene flow, the "flow of alleles in and out of a population due to... migration" (lines 23385-23386), an unrelated force not operating in this closed scenario. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that selection acts on whole-organism net fitness to a described linkage scenario to explain why a beneficial allele is lost.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
