-- Q7 [Natural Selection & Fitness] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Natural Selection & Fitness', 'In a meadow population of wildflowers, taller plants reliably produce more seeds, yet measurements show that an individual plant''s height is determined almost entirely by the richness of the soil patch it grows in rather than by differences among the plants'' alleles. Even though the tall plants out-reproduce the short ones each season, the population''s average height does NOT shift over generations. The most direct reason natural selection fails to drive height evolution here is that the height variation', '[{"label": "A", "text": "is not advantageous in the current environment"}, {"label": "B", "text": "arose only in response to the changing soil"}, {"label": "C", "text": "lacks an underlying genetic basis"}, {"label": "D", "text": "occurs in too small a population"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is C because natural selection can change a population across generations only when the phenotypic variation it acts on has a genetic basis; variation produced by nongenetic causes, such as differences in soil nutrition, cannot be passed to offspring, so even strong differential reproduction leaves the next generation''s trait distribution unchanged. OpenStax states that for selection to act, differences among individuals "must have some genetic basis; otherwise, the selection will not lead to change in the next generation," and explicitly gives the example of "an individual''s height because of better nutrition rather than different genes" (Biology 2e, lines 21762-21765). The text likewise notes that a changed environment produces change in the population "if the characteristics are genetically determined" (line 21996) and that selection "can only act on heritable traits" (line 22906). Because the tall plants'' advantage rests on soil, not alleles, their abundant seeds do not inherit tallness, and average height stays put. (Choice A) is incorrect: the scenario states tall plants reliably out-reproduce short ones, so the trait IS advantageous; the limiting factor is heritability, not fitness, and selection acts on existing variation regardless of whether every variant is beneficial (lines 21762-21763). (Choice B) is incorrect: variation that selection works on "is already in a population and does not arise in response to an environmental change," as with antibiotic-resistance alleles that predate the antibiotic (lines 21998-22005); even if soil-driven height did arise in response to soil, that misstates the actual barrier, which is the absence of a genetic basis. (Choice C) is correct: only heritable, genetically based variation can respond to selection, and nutrition-driven height is the textbook''s own example of variation lacking that basis (lines 21762-21765, 22906-22911). (Choice D) is incorrect: small population size magnifies random genetic drift, a different and non-selective force, rather than explaining why selection cannot act on a non-genetic trait (lines 22807-22811); population size is not the issue described. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that selection requires heritable variation to a described scenario and reason from the soil-driven, non-genetic cause of the variation to the conclusion that the population cannot evolve.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
