-- Q8 [Natural Selection & Fitness] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Natural Selection & Fitness', 'A population of bark beetles currently shows a rising frequency of a dark (melanic) allele because soot-darkened trunks let predatory birds easily pick off the lighter beetles. A student concludes that the lineage is steadily progressing toward a permanently superior, optimally camouflaged form. This reasoning is flawed because natural selection in this population can only do which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Generate new camouflage alleles when needed"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Increase the population''s absolute fitness ceiling"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Act directly on individual advantageous alleles"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Track the present environment using existing variation"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is D because natural selection is not goal-directed: it does not move a lineage toward a preconceived ''superior'' or ''perfect'' form, and it can only favor variants already present that maximize reproduction in the environment ''at a particular time.'' OpenStax states selection lets species ''simply track their changing environment with adaptations that maximize their reproduction in a particular environment at a particular time,'' and that ''a trait that fits in one environment at one time may well be fatal at some point in the future'' (Biology 2e, lines 22007-22013). The melanic allele is favored only because the trunks are currently sooty; if the soot cleared, that same allele could become disadvantageous, so there is no trajectory toward a permanent optimum. (Choice A) is incorrect because selection ''does not create anything from scratch'' and is ''limited by a population''s existing genetic variability''; new alleles arise via mutation and gene flow, and mutations for resistance ''do not arise as a result'' of the selective pressure (lines 22000-22005, 23318-23320) — this is the misconception that the environment calls forth needed variation. (Choice B) is incorrect because what matters is relative fitness, ''not an individual''s absolute fitness... but rather how it compares to the other organisms in the population'' (lines 23158-23160); selection has no absolute ceiling it climbs toward, and the textbook explicitly says it ''cannot produce the perfect organism'' (line 23317). (Choice C) is incorrect because ''Natural selection acts on entire organisms, not on an individual allele within the organism'' — it ''works at the individual, not allele level,'' so good alleles can be lost and bad alleles kept depending on the organism''s net fitness (lines 23151, 23322-23327). (Choice D) is correct: selection acts only on the variation already in the population and tracks the current environment, with no built-in direction toward improvement or perfection. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that selection is non-goal-directed and environment-tracking to diagnose the error in a described ''progress toward perfection'' scenario.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
