-- Q9 [Modes of Selection] easy skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Modes of Selection', 'A population of woodland mice lives on a forest floor that is a uniform, consistent shade of brown. Over many generations, the distribution of coat color narrows: very light and very dark mice both become rare, while medium-brown mice that match the floor make up an increasing majority. This pattern is most consistent with which mode of selection?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Directional selection"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Diversifying selection"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Stabilizing selection"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Genetic drift"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is C because the described distribution favors the average (medium-brown) phenotype while selecting against both the light and dark extremes, which is the definition of stabilizing selection. OpenStax states that "If natural selection favors an average phenotype, selecting against extreme variation, the population will undergo stabilizing selection," and uses this exact woodland-mouse example: mice "whose fur is most closely matched to that color will be most likely to survive and reproduce," while mice "a bit lighter or a bit darker will stand out against the ground and be more likely to fall victim to predation," so that "the population''s genetic variability will decrease." The glossary defines stabilizing selection as "selection that favors average phenotypes." (Choice A) is incorrect because directional selection "favors phenotypes at one end of the spectrum of existing variation" (e.g., the peppered moth shifting toward the melanic form), which would shift the peak toward one extreme, not narrow it around the middle. (Choice B) is incorrect because diversifying selection is "selection that favors two or more distinct phenotypes" while "intermediate phenotypes are, on average, less fit," producing increased variability and the opposite of the pattern shown. (Choice C) is correct: only stabilizing selection favors the intermediate phenotype, selects against both extremes, and decreases genetic variability, matching the narrowing distribution around medium-brown. (Choice D) is incorrect because genetic drift is "effect of chance on a population''s gene pool," a random, non-selective process; here the consistent fitness advantage of the matching phenotype, driven by predation, indicates non-random selection rather than chance. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of each selection mode to a described phenotype distribution to infer which mechanism produced it.', 'easy', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
