-- Q10 [Modes of Selection] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Modes of Selection', 'In a forest near nineteenth-century English factories where soot progressively darkens the tree trunks on which peppered moths rest, a researcher records the frequency of the light and dark (melanic) wing forms across five generations, as shown below.

| Generation | Light form | Melanic form |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 0.90 | 0.10 |
| 2 | 0.72 | 0.28 |
| 3 | 0.55 | 0.45 |
| 4 | 0.38 | 0.62 |
| 5 | 0.21 | 0.79 |

Given that predatory birds spot light moths most easily against the darkened bark, the mode of selection that BEST accounts for this trend is:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Directional selection"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Stabilizing selection"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Diversifying selection"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Genetic drift"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is A because the data show one extreme phenotype (the melanic form) steadily increasing from 0.10 to 0.79 while the opposite extreme declines, which is the signature of directional selection. OpenStax states that directional selection ''selects for phenotypes at one end of the spectrum of existing variation'' and uses this exact peppered-moth scenario: ''as soot began spewing from factories, the trees darkened... the frequency of the moth''s melanic form increased because they had a higher survival rate.'' The result ''is a shift in the population''s genetic variability toward the new, fit phenotype.'' Because birds preferentially eat the more visible light moths, survival is non-random and fitness-based, ruling out chance. (Choice A) is correct: the population''s distribution shifts toward one favored extreme as the environment changes, matching the directional-selection definition. (Choice B) is wrong because stabilizing selection ''favors an average phenotype, selecting against extreme variation'' and decreases variability rather than driving the population toward one extreme. (Choice C) is wrong because diversifying selection ''favors two or more distinct phenotypes'' at both ends while selecting against intermediates and increases genetic variability, but here only the melanic extreme is favored. (Choice D) is wrong because genetic drift ''stems from the chance occurrence that some individuals... have more offspring than others''; the consistent, predation-driven directional trend reflects differential fitness, not random sampling. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of the modes of selection to a described phenotype-distribution trend and infer the underlying mechanism.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
