-- Q11 [Modes of Selection] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Modes of Selection', 'In a beach population of mice, light-colored individuals blend in with the open sand and dark-colored individuals hide in patches of tall grass, while medium-colored mice match neither background and are eaten by predators. Over generations, both the light and the dark extremes increase in frequency. This pattern is an example of which mode of natural selection?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Stabilizing selection"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Diversifying selection"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Directional selection"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Frequency-dependent selection"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is B because diversifying selection favors two or more distinct, extreme phenotypes while selecting against the intermediate (average) phenotype, which increases the population''s genetic variability. OpenStax describes exactly this beach-mouse scenario: "light-colored mice that blend in with the sand would be favored, as well as dark-colored mice that can hide in the grass. Medium-colored mice, alternatively would not blend in... and thus predators would most likely eat them. The result of this type of selection is increased genetic variability as the population becomes more diverse." The text further states that "diversifying selection results in increased genetic variability by selecting for two or more distinct phenotypes." Because both extremes (not one end, and not the average) are favored here, the pattern is diversifying selection. (Choice A) is wrong because stabilizing selection "favors an average phenotype, selecting against extreme variation," producing a decrease in variability — the opposite of what is described. (Choice B) is correct: two extreme phenotypes are selected for while the intermediate is selected against, increasing variability. (Choice C) is wrong because directional selection "selects for phenotypes at one end of the spectrum," shifting the population toward a single new phenotype, whereas here both ends are favored. (Choice D) is wrong because frequency-dependent selection "favors phenotypes that are either common... or rare," basing fitness on how common a phenotype is rather than on two fixed extreme phenotypes matching two backgrounds. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definition of diversifying selection and distinguish it from the other modes of natural selection.', 'easy', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
