-- Q12 [Modes of Selection] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Modes of Selection', 'A beach mouse population lives where light-colored sand is interspersed with patches of tall dark grass. Predators readily catch and eat medium-colored mice, while light mice that blend with the sand and dark mice that hide in the grass both survive at high rates and reproduce. Over many generations, the consequence of this selection pattern for the population is:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "decreased variability via stabilizing selection"}, {"label": "B", "text": "increased variability via genetic drift"}, {"label": "C", "text": "a directional shift toward dark coats"}, {"label": "D", "text": "increased variability via diversifying selection"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is D because the scenario describes two distinct extreme phenotypes (light and dark coats) each being favored while the intermediate (medium) phenotype is selected against. OpenStax names this diversifying selection, defined as "selection that favors two or more distinct phenotypes," and states that for exactly this beach-mouse case "the result of this type of selection is increased genetic variability as the population becomes more diverse." The chapter summary reinforces that "diversifying selection results in increased genetic variability by selecting for two or more distinct phenotypes." Because both ends of the spectrum are fit and the middle is eaten, the population spreads into two forms rather than converging. (Choice A) is wrong because stabilizing selection "favors an average phenotype, selecting against extreme variation," producing decreased variability — the opposite of the medium-eaten pattern here. (Choice B) is wrong because the differential survival is fitness-based and NON-random (predators target the conspicuous medium mice), whereas genetic drift "stems from the chance occurrence" of which individuals reproduce; drift is random, not driven by camouflage advantage. (Choice C) is wrong because directional selection "selects for phenotypes at one end of the spectrum," shifting the population toward a single new form, but here both the light and dark ends are favored, not one. (Choice D) is correct: two extreme phenotypes are each favored while the average is selected against, yielding increased genetic variability — the textbook''s definition and stated outcome of diversifying selection. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining features of each selection mode to a described phenotype-distribution and infer both the correct mode and its variability outcome.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
