-- Q13 [Modes of Selection] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Modes of Selection', 'A single seed-eating bird species colonizes a remote volcanic archipelago, and over time its descendants spread across the islands, where separate lineages evolve thicker beaks for cracking hard seeds, long beaks for probing flowers, and sharp beaks for spearing insects, ultimately forming many distinct species. This pattern of one founder species giving rise to numerous species, each fitted to a previously unoccupied niche, is best classified as which evolutionary phenomenon?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Adaptive radiation"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Directional selection"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Allopatric speciation"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Diversifying selection"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is A because adaptive radiation is the process in which a population of one species disperses, each lineage finds a distinct niche or isolated habitat, and the varied demands of these new lifestyles drive multiple speciation events originating from a single founder species. OpenStax states, "In some cases, a population of one species disperses throughout an area, and each finds a distinct niche or isolated habitat. Over time, the varied demands of their new lifestyles lead to multiple speciation events originating from a single species. We call this adaptive radiation because many adaptations evolve from a single point of origin," and names Darwin''s finches and the Hawaiian honeycreepers as examples (lines 22166-22185). The defining signature here is one-to-many diversification into separate niches, which distinguishes adaptive radiation from selection acting within a single population. (Choice A) is correct: the scenario describes a single founder species radiating into many species, each adapted to a distinct open niche, which is precisely adaptive radiation. (Choice B) Directional selection "selects for phenotypes at one end of the spectrum of existing variation" within one population, such as the peppered moth shifting toward the melanic form (lines 23177-23184); it shifts allele frequencies in a single population rather than generating many new species in different niches. (Choice C) Allopatric speciation is "speciation that occurs via geographic separation" (lines 22473-22474) and may be a mechanism contributing to the divergence, but it describes the geographic isolation step, not the overall macro-pattern of one species branching into many niche-specialized species. (Choice D) Diversifying (disruptive) selection favors two or more distinct phenotypes while selecting against intermediate phenotypes within a single population (lines 23195-23197, 23374); it acts on the variation inside one population and does not by itself denote one founder species radiating into multiple species across distinct habitats. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definition of adaptive radiation and distinguish it from individual modes of selection and from a single isolation mechanism.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
