-- Q14 [Modes of Selection] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Modes of Selection', 'On an island, researchers track a seed-eating bird population through a drought that eliminates small soft seeds and leaves mostly large hard seeds. Across the next three generations, the beak-depth distribution shifts steadily from a peak near 9 mm toward a new peak near 11 mm, with deep-billed birds showing the highest survival on the remaining seeds. This generational trend is MOST consistent with which process?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Stabilizing selection on beak depth"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Genetic drift acting on beak depth"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Directional selection favoring deeper beaks"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Diversifying selection on beak depth"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is C because the described trend has the two signatures of directional selection: an environmental change (loss of small seeds) and a fitness difference correlated with phenotype (deep-billed birds survive best), driving the phenotype distribution toward one end of the existing range. OpenStax states that ''When the environment changes, populations will often undergo directional selection, which selects for phenotypes at one end of the spectrum of existing variation,'' with ''a shift in the population''s genetic variability toward the new, fit phenotype'' (lines 23177-23187), and the glossary defines directional selection as ''selection that favors phenotypes at one end of the spectrum of existing variation'' (lines 23371-23373). Because the shift is non-random and tied to both an environmental change and a survival advantage, it is selection rather than chance. (Choice A) is incorrect: stabilizing selection ''favors average phenotypes'' and selects against extremes, decreasing variation around an unchanged mean (lines 23395, 23168-23175), but here the mean itself moves to a new value. (Choice B) is incorrect: in genetic drift ''allele frequencies within a population change randomly with no advantage,'' and it is ''the effect of chance on a population''s gene pool'' (lines 22806-22808, 23389-23390); the data show a fitness advantage and an environmental driver, so the change is not random. (Choice C) is correct: the environment changed, deep-billed birds had higher survival, and the distribution shifted toward one extreme—exactly directional selection (lines 23177-23187). (Choice D) is incorrect: diversifying selection ''favors two or more distinct phenotypes'' while intermediate phenotypes are less fit, increasing variability (lines 23374, 23195-23211), yet only one extreme (deep beaks) is favored here, not both. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of selection modes and drift to a described phenotype-distribution trend to infer the responsible mechanism.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
