-- Q16 [Speciation & Isolation] medium skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Speciation & Isolation', 'Two related frog species inhabit the same pond, but one breeds only from January to March while the other breeds only from March to May, so the two never attempt to mate at the same time. This difference in breeding schedules functions as which type of reproductive barrier?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A gametic barrier"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A prezygotic barrier"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A postzygotic barrier"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Hybrid inviability"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is B because differences in breeding schedules are called temporal isolation, which the textbook lists as a form of prezygotic barrier. A prezygotic barrier is a mechanism that blocks reproduction from taking place before a zygote (fertilized egg) forms, and the staggered breeding seasons prevent the two frog species from ever mating, so no fertilization can occur. The textbook states: "Differences in breeding schedules, which we call temporal isolation, can act as a form of reproductive isolation," and "a prezygotic barrier is a mechanism that blocks reproduction from taking place... before zygote formation" (OpenStax Biology 2e, lines 22270-22278). (Choice A) A gametic barrier is a prezygotic barrier, but it specifically occurs when individuals do mate yet "differences in their gamete cells (eggs and sperm) prevent fertilization" (lines 22493-22496); here mating never happens at all because of timing, not because of gamete incompatibility. (Choice B) is correct: temporal isolation, a difference in breeding schedules, acts as a prezygotic barrier because it prevents fertilization before any zygote can form. (Choice C) A postzygotic barrier is a "reproductive isolation mechanism that occurs after zygote formation" (lines 22475-22476); timing differences act before fertilization, so no zygote ever forms. (Choice D) Hybrid inviability is a postzygotic outcome in which a hybrid zygote forms but "the hybrid organisms simply are not viable" (lines 22333-22339); it requires a zygote, which never forms when breeding seasons do not overlap. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that temporal isolation is classified as a prezygotic barrier that blocks fertilization before zygote formation.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
