-- Q18 [Speciation & Isolation] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Speciation & Isolation', 'A river permanently changes course and splits a single population of ground squirrels into two groups that can no longer reach each other. Over many generations the two groups are observed to accumulate increasingly different allele frequencies at numerous genetic loci. In this scenario, the geographic barrier most directly drives divergence by doing which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Halting gene flow between the two groups"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Generating new adaptive mutations on demand"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Producing polyploid offspring within one group"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Establishing a postzygotic reproductive barrier"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is A because allopatric speciation begins when a geographic discontinuity blocks the free movement of alleles between populations. OpenStax states that in a continuous population "Gene flow, the movement of alleles across a species'' range, is relatively free," but "When populations become geographically discontinuous, it prevents alleles'' free-flow," and "When that separation lasts for a period of time, the two populations are able to evolve along different trajectories" so that "their allele frequencies at numerous genetic loci gradually become increasingly different as new alleles independently arise by mutation in each population" (lines 22117-22123). The barrier itself does not create mutations or impose a reproductive barrier; it simply stops gene flow, which then permits independent divergence. (Choice A) is correct: halting gene flow is the direct effect of the geographic barrier, and it is the loss of this "free-flow" of alleles that allows the two isolated populations to diverge independently (lines 22117-22122). (Choice B) is wrong: mutations "independently arise" in each population over time but are random events, not generated on demand by the barrier; the text does not describe the barrier producing adaptive mutations (lines 22122-22123). (Choice C) is wrong: polyploidy is a chromosomal error that drives sympatric speciation "within the same space," not the allopatric mechanism caused by a geographic barrier (lines 22194-22201). (Choice D) is wrong: a postzygotic barrier is a "reproductive isolation mechanism that occurs after zygote formation," an eventual outcome of prolonged divergence rather than the direct, immediate effect of the river splitting the population (lines 22475-22476). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that allopatric speciation is initiated by geographic separation halting gene flow, allowing isolated populations to diverge along independent trajectories.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
