-- Q19 [Speciation & Isolation] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Speciation & Isolation', 'Two closely related insect species coexist in one habitat, and biologists document three barriers that keep them reproductively isolated. Consider the following barriers.

I. Males of one species flash a species-specific light pattern that females of the other species do NOT recognize, so the two never attempt to mate.
II. When the species do cross-mate, the resulting hybrid embryos fail to develop and die before birth.
III. Differences in the egg and sperm cells prevent fertilization even when individuals of the two species attempt to mate.

Which of the barriers act as prezygotic isolation?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is C because a prezygotic barrier is a reproductive isolation mechanism that occurs before zygote formation, blocking mating or fertilization, whereas a postzygotic barrier occurs after the zygote forms. Barrier I is behavioral isolation, a prezygotic barrier in which a specific behavior (a species-specific light display, like that of male fireflies) prevents reproduction from taking place. Barrier III is a gametic barrier, a prezygotic barrier occurring when closely related individuals mate but differences in their gamete cells (eggs and sperm) prevent fertilization. Barrier II is hybrid inviability, a postzygotic barrier in which the hybrid organisms are not viable and do not survive past the embryonic stages, so it does not belong with the prezygotic group. (Choice A) I only is a partial answer that correctly tags the behavioral display as prezygotic but overlooks that the gametic barrier (III) also blocks fertilization before any zygote forms. (Choice B) III only correctly identifies the gametic barrier but ignores that behavioral isolation (I) likewise prevents mating before fertilization. (Choice C) is correct: I (behavioral isolation) and III (gametic barrier) both act before zygote formation, while II (hybrid inviability) acts after the zygote forms. (Choice D) II and III only reverses the placement of the behavioral display and incorrectly groups the postzygotic hybrid inviability (II) with the prezygotic gametic barrier; hybrid inviability occurs only after fertilization. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the prezygotic-versus-postzygotic distinction to classify each described barrier by whether it acts before or after zygote formation.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
