-- Q20 [Speciation & Isolation] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Speciation & Isolation', 'In a single lake, a population of cichlid fish splits as one group begins feeding on a food source at a greater depth, and these fish gradually breed mostly among themselves while continuing to share the lake with the original population. The two groups develop distinct morphologies despite the absence of any physical barrier between them. This divergence is an example of which process?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Allopatric speciation"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Sympatric speciation"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Diversifying selection"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Genetic drift"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is B because speciation that occurs within the same geographic space, without any physical barrier separating the diverging groups, is sympatric speciation. The textbook describes exactly this scenario: a lake fish population in which a group exploits a new food resource at a different depth, interacts and breeds mostly among themselves, and accumulates genetic differences leading to sympatric speciation, with Lake Victoria cichlids cited as a famous real example (lines 22344-22358, 22192-22196). Because the cichlids never become geographically separated, the process is sympatric ("same homeland") rather than allopatric. (Choice A) Allopatric speciation "occurs via geographic separation" (line 22473-22474); here the fish remain in one lake with no physical barrier, so this is the reversed condition. (Choice B) is correct: sympatric speciation is "speciation occurring within a parent species remaining in one location," which the resource-based cichlid divergence illustrates (lines 22109-22110, 22344-22358). (Choice C) Diversifying selection "favors two or more distinct phenotypes" within a population (lines 23195-23197, 23374); it is a mode of natural selection acting on a trait distribution, not the name for the splitting of one population into reproductively isolated species. (Choice D) Genetic drift is the "effect of chance on a population''s gene pool," whereas this divergence is driven by resource use and non-random mating, not chance sampling. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of sympatric speciation to a described ecological scenario and distinguish it from allopatric speciation, a selection mode, and a random drift mechanism.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
