-- Q21 [Evolutionary Patterns] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Evolutionary Patterns', 'Bats and insects both possess wings that function in flight, yet these wings arose from entirely different ancestral structures in lineages that do not share recent common ancestry. This pattern, in which similar adaptive traits arise independently, is best characterized as which evolutionary phenomenon?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Divergent evolution"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Homologous structures"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Vestigial structures"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Convergent evolution"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is D because convergent evolution is the process in which similar traits evolve independently in species that do not share a recent common ancestry. OpenStax states, "flight has evolved in both bats and insects, and they both have structures we refer to as wings... However, bat and insect wings have evolved from very different original structures. We call this phenomenon convergent evolution, where similar traits evolve independently in species that do not share a recent common ancestry." Because the wings serve the same function (flight) but arose from different developmental origins, they are analogous structures, the hallmark product of convergent evolution. (Choice A) Divergent evolution is the opposite pattern: it describes "two species that evolve in diverse directions from a common point," so it requires a shared ancestor and does not fit independently evolved wings. (Choice B) Homologous structures are inherited from a common ancestor and stem from shared developmental origins; bat and insect wings are explicitly analogous, not homologous, because their embryonic origins differ. (Choice C) Vestigial structures are "unused structures without function" that are residual from a past common ancestor, whereas functional wings used for flight are not vestigial. (Choice D) is correct: bat versus insect wings is the textbook example of convergent evolution producing analogous structures with similar function in distantly related, independently evolving lineages. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definition of convergent evolution and distinguish it from divergent evolution, homology, and vestigiality.', 'easy', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
