-- Q22 [Evolutionary Patterns] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Evolutionary Patterns', 'Several flowering-plant lineages descend from one common ancestor and share the same basic floral anatomy, yet their reproductive organs now look strikingly different after each lineage adapted to a distinct pollinator and physical environment. This pattern of accumulating differences in related lineages is termed:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Divergent evolution"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Convergent evolution"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Adaptive radiation"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Coevolution"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is A because divergent evolution is the process by which groups of organisms that share a common ancestor evolve in diverse directions from that common point, accumulating differences as selection acts on them in different environments. The textbook defines it with this exact example: "We can see such divergent evolution in the forms of the reproductive organs of flowering plants which share the same basic anatomies; however, they can look very different as a result of selection in different physical environments and adaptation to different kinds of pollinators." The glossary restates it as the "process by which groups of organisms evolve in diverse directions from a common point." (Choice A) is correct: the lineages share recent common ancestry (same basic floral anatomy) and have moved apart in form, which is the defining signature of divergent evolution. (Choice B) is wrong because convergent evolution is "where similar traits evolve independently in species that do not share a recent common ancestry"; here the lineages DO share a recent common ancestor and are becoming more different, not more similar, so it is the reverse situation. (Choice C) is wrong because adaptive radiation is "speciation when one species radiates to form several other species," emphasizing a burst of multiple speciation events filling distinct niches (e.g., Hawaiian honeycreepers); the prompt highlights accumulating morphological differences in already-related lineages, not the rapid multiplication of species into new niches. (Choice D) is wrong because coevolution refers to reciprocal evolutionary change between two interacting groups (such as flowering plants and their insect pollinators each shaping the other); the stem describes one ancestral plant lineage diverging, not two interacting groups exerting selection on each other. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the textbook definition of divergent evolution and distinguish it from related macroevolutionary pattern terms.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
