-- Q23 [Evolutionary Patterns] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Evolutionary Patterns', 'A comparative anatomist notes that the bones forming the wings of a bird and a bat are arranged in the same basic forelimb pattern inherited from a shared tetrapod ancestor, yet the flight surfaces of the two wings developed from completely different tissues after the lineages diverged. Considering both observations together, this single appendage is best characterized as:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "homologous in bones and in flight surface"}, {"label": "B", "text": "homologous in bones but analogous in flight surface"}, {"label": "C", "text": "analogous in bones but homologous in flight surface"}, {"label": "D", "text": "vestigial in bones and analogous in flight surface"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is B because the same appendage can simultaneously contain homologous and analogous features at different levels of organization. The textbook states that ''the bird and bat wings are homologous because the bones are inherited from a common ancestor, while the wings themselves are analogous as they evolved independently'' (lines 23839-23840). Homologous structures share a similar embryonic origin and shared ancestry, whereas analogous organs share only a similar function arising from convergent evolution under similar environmental constraints (lines 23842-23843). The bones trace to the common tetrapod forelimb (homology), but the flight surfaces arose separately in each lineage (analogy by convergence). (Choice A) is incorrect because the flight surfaces did not arise from a common ancestral wing; the wings ''evolved independently,'' making them analogous rather than homologous (lines 23839-23840). (Choice B) is correct: the shared inherited bone arrangement is homologous, while the independently evolved flight surfaces are analogous, exactly the dual characterization the textbook gives for bird and bat wings (lines 23839-23840). (Choice C) reverses the relationship; the bones (inherited from a common ancestor) are the homologous component and the flight surfaces (evolved independently) are the analogous component, not the other way around (lines 23839-23840). (Choice D) is incorrect because vestigial structures are ''unused structures without function'' that are residual parts of a past common ancestor, such as hind leg bones in whales (lines 21861-21862); the wing bones are fully functional, not vestigial. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the homology-versus-analogy framework to a described anatomical scenario and resolve how one appendage can be both at different structural levels.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
