-- Q24 [Evolutionary Patterns] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Evolutionary Patterns', 'In a laboratory experiment, *Paramecium aurelia* and *Paramecium caudatum* each grow well when cultured alone, but when the two species are combined in a single test tube sharing the same food supply, *P. caudatum* declines and is eliminated. Which of the following statements about this outcome are consistent with the competitive exclusion principle?

I. The two species CANNOT indefinitely coexist while competing for all the same resources.
II. *P. caudatum* was eliminated because random fluctuations in allele frequencies reduced its population.
III. Coexistence could be restored if one species evolved to use a different resource or feeding time.', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is C because the competitive exclusion principle states that two species cannot occupy the same niche in a habitat, so they cannot coexist if they compete for all the same resources (Statement I), as demonstrated when P. aurelia outcompetes P. caudatum for food and drives it to extinction. The textbook also states that this exclusion may be avoided if a population evolves to use a different resource, area, or feeding time, called resource partitioning, allowing coexistence in different microniches (Statement III). The textbook attributes the displacement of P. caudatum specifically to competition for food (a fitness-based, non-random process), not to random change. (Choice A) I only is incomplete because it omits the textbook''s explicit statement that resource partitioning can permit coexistence, making III also correct. (Choice B) III only is incomplete because Statement I directly restates the competitive exclusion principle, which the textbook affirms. (Choice C) is correct: the textbook states that two species cannot occupy the same niche (I) and that exclusion may be avoided through resource partitioning if a population evolves to use a different resource, area, or feeding time (III) — "These organisms coexist by minimizing direct competition." (Choice D) II and III only is wrong because Statement II misattributes the outcome to random fluctuations in allele frequencies (genetic drift), whereas the textbook attributes the elimination to P. aurelia outcompeting P. caudatum for food — a non-random, fitness-driven competitive process, not chance. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the competitive exclusion principle to a described experimental outcome and distinguish competition-driven exclusion from random drift.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
